    "id": "F-16",
    "title": "No multi-stage build; build toolchain and sources ship to production",
    "severity": "Low",
    "category": "Attack Surface Reduction",
    "cwe": "CWE-668",
    "location": "Dockerfile:1-11 (single-stage build)",
    "description": "The Dockerfile builds and runs in one stage on a full Debian-based Node image. The production image therefore contains the compiler toolchain, package manager caches, headers, devDependencies, tests and any build artifacts, and it needs write access during install. Layer order also puts COPY . . before npm install, so every source change re-runs the full dependency install.",
    "impact": "A larger image means more packages with more CVEs, more binaries an attacker can reuse after gaining a foothold (compilers to build exploits, curl/wget for exfiltration), and slower, less auditable deploys. It also weakens the read-only root filesystem control in F-11.",
    "recommendation": "Split into a build stage that installs all dependencies and compiles assets, and a minimal runtime stage that copies only node_modules and the source it needs. Use a slim or distroless base, order layers so dependencies are cached separately from source, and confirm the final image contains no compiler or package manager.",
    "fix": "FROM node:22-bookworm-slim AS build\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY package*.json ./\nRUN npm ci\nCOPY . .\nRUN npm run build\n\nFROM node:22-bookworm-slim AS runtime\nWORKDIR /app\nENV NODE_ENV=production\nCOPY --from=build /app/node_modules ./node_modules\nCOPY --from=build /app/dist ./dist\nUSER app\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]",
    "references": [
     "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/668.html",
     "https://docs.docker.com/build/building/multi-stage/",
     "https://github.com/GoogleContainerTools/distroless"
    ]
